@* Button's Overview, written by hand: the generated parts (mode table, parameters) are placed
   where they read best. *@

<ExampleViewer Example="button-variants" SourceOpen />

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

<Heading Level="2">Accents and variants</Heading>
<Prose>
    <p>
        <code>Accent</code> picks the color: <code>Primary</code> (the default) for the main action, <code>Neutral</code>
        for the rest, <code>Danger</code> for destructive ones, or any color with <code>Accent.Of("#0f766e")</code>.
        <code>Variant</code> sets the weight: <code>Solid</code>, <code>Soft</code>, <code>Outline</code>, <code>Ghost</code>
        or <code>Link</code>. Keep one solid primary button per area, so the main action is obvious.
    </p>
</Prose>

<Heading Level="2">Submit and reset</Heading>
<Prose>
    <p>
        A button does nothing by itself. Inside a <a href="docs/components/form">Form</a>, <code>Submit</code> posts the
        form and <code>Reset</code> puts the fields back. Neither needs a circuit or JavaScript: the browser does the
        work, so the same form posts on a static page and validates live on an interactive one.
    </p>
</Prose>
<ExampleViewer Example="button-submit-reset" />
<Prose>
    <p>
        Several submit buttons in one form can say which one was pressed: <code>Name</code> and <code>Value</code>
        post with the form, like the HTML attributes. The
        <a href="demos/button/several-submit-buttons">Several submit buttons</a> demo shows one handler telling
        Publish, Save draft and Discard apart. <code>Form</code> and <code>FormAction</code> submit a form elsewhere
        on the page, or to another URL.
    </p>
</Prose>

<Heading Level="2">Links</Heading>
<Prose>
    <p>
        With <code>Href</code> the button renders an <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> that looks the same, for navigation.
        <code>External</code> opens a new tab, adds an icon and tells screen readers. A link can't be <code>Submit</code>
        or <code>Reset</code>, or take <code>Name</code>, <code>Value</code>, <code>Form</code> or <code>FormAction</code>:
        that throws, so the mistake shows up the first time the page renders.
    </p>
</Prose>
<ExampleViewer Example="button-links" />

<Heading Level="2">Sizes and icons</Heading>
<Prose>
    <p>
        <code>Size</code> is a <code>ControlSize</code>, shared with fields, tabs and pagination, so a button lines up
        with the input beside it. <code>StartIcon</code> and <code>EndIcon</code> take any icon, sized to the button.
    </p>
</Prose>
<ExampleViewer Example="button-sizes-icons" />

<Heading Level="2">Loading and disabled</Heading>
<Prose>
    <p>
        <code>Loading</code> shows a spinner in place of the start icon and blocks clicks, but keeps the button
        focusable and its label readable; screen readers hear <code>LoadingLabel</code>. <code>Disabled</code> uses the
        native attribute on a button. A disabled or loading link loses its <code>href</code>, keeps
        <code>role="link"</code> and sets <code>aria-disabled</code>.
    </p>
</Prose>
<ExampleViewer Example="button-states" />

<Heading Level="2">Running C# on click</Heading>
<Prose>
    <p>
        <code>OnClick</code> runs C# on an interactive page (Server, WebAssembly or Auto). A static page has no
        connection to run it over, so there it does nothing: switch this example to Off or Static and the clicks don't
        count. For actions that must work everywhere, use <code>Submit</code> in a form or <code>Href</code>.
    </p>
</Prose>
<ExampleViewer Example="button-on-click" />

<Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
<List Gap="2">
    <ListItem>It renders a native <Code>button</Code> or <Code>a</Code>, so Enter, Space and focus work as the browser defines them.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>The focus ring shows for keyboard focus only.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>An action shown only as an icon needs a name: use <Anchor Href="docs/components/icon-button">IconButton</Anchor>, whose <Code>Label</Code> is required.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>A loading button keeps focus and sets <Code>aria-busy</Code>, so a keyboard user isn't dropped back to the top of the page.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>An external link says it opens a new tab (<Code>NewTabLabel</Code>), not just with its icon.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" />

